  "relevance_note": "This CVID methylation study reports one discovery-cohort patient carrying a TNFAIP3 variant (\"one CVID patient was later found to carry a monogenic TNFAIP3 variant\"), but gives no variant description and no individual-level clinical detail.",
  "method_note": "Read the full paper by hand; the JSON was assembled with a short Python script in the scratch directory from values I chose while reading. No table was transcribed: Tables 1 and 2 give only aggregate cohort data. Only one person is extracted: the CD14+ discovery-cohort CVID patient the paper calls 'the TNFAIP3 carrier' (also 'this individual', 'this patient'); the paper gives no other label. The other five discovery-cohort CVID patients (no monogenic variants identified) and the 60-patient plasma cohort are described only in aggregate and carry no reported TNFAIP3 variant, so they are not extracted. The variant itself is not described: no HGVS, transcript, zygosity, inheritance or parental testing is given, so variant_original_string is 'not reported'. The paper says 'This TNFAIP3 variant was first described in a CVID patient in 2020 (34)'; it does not say whether that is the same patient, so cites_prior_report_of_same_patients is left null rather than true. Sex and age of the carrier cannot be derived (Table 1 CD14 CVID group: 3 patients, 1 female, mean age 35, range 22-46). Group statements applied to the carrier because they name the whole group of six discovery-cohort CVID patients: complicated CVID subgroup, no acute infection at inclusion, no immunosuppression apart from Ig replacement. The non-infectious complication definition listing specific complications is given for the plasma cohort, and the carrier's specific complications are not stated, so all 20 checklist items are 'not reported'. 'All surviving patients' (2025 review) implies some discovery-cohort deaths, but the carrier's vital status is not stated. Enrolment was at Oslo University Hospital, but ancestry/country of origin is not stated. The authors' diagnosis for this person is CVID, not A20 haploinsufficiency. Pathogenicity is recorded as the authors' word 'monogenic'; the sentence 'No other CVID patients in this cohort had pathogenic variants detected by the gene panel' implies, but does not directly state, that the TNFAIP3 variant was considered pathogenic. The functional_evidence_statement is a DNA methylation pathway analysis of the carrier's monocytes, not a variant-specific functional assay. No contradictions were found in the paper (the Table 2 footnote repeats a phrase, apparently a conversion artefact).",
